// Python — a rigorous, first-principles treatment of the LANGUAGE MODEL rather
// than a tour of the standard library. The spine is CPython's own vocabulary:
// every value is an object with identity, type, and value; every name is a
// binding in a namespace, resolved by rules fixed at compile time; every
// syntactic form (a + b, for x in xs, with f as g, obj.attr, await c) is sugar
// over a documented protocol dispatched on the *type*; and every execution is a
// stack of frames whose lifetimes explain closures, generators, and coroutines
// alike. Units derive consequences from that model and prove what is provable:
// the hash/eq invariant that dict correctness rests on, the UnboundLocalError
// rule, late binding of cells, the attribute-lookup precedence theorem, C3
// linearization and its monotonicity guarantee, the with-statement release
// theorem, amortized O(1) append, Timsort's stability, the non-atomicity of
// read-modify-write under the GIL, and the contravariance of function subtyping.
//
// Grounded in the primary sources: the Python Language Reference (Data Model,
// Execution Model, Expressions), the PEPs that introduced each mechanism, the
// CPython implementation notes (listobject.c, dictobject.c, listsort.txt), the
// C3 linearization paper, Liskov & Wing on behavioral subtyping, and Ramalho's
// Fluent Python for the idiom layer. Where the course teaches judgment rather
// than derivation (data-structure choice, concurrency model selection, test
// design), gates use AI-graded `open` questions under a demanding reviewer.
//
// Units are inserted one at a time via scripts/insert-unit.mjs — never
// generated in one shot (see AUTHORING.md).
//
// Outline (10 units):
//   u1  Objects, Names, and Binding   — identity/type/value, aliasing, eq/hash, copies
//   u2  Namespaces, Scope, Closures   — compile-time scope, cells, late binding, modules
//   u3  The Data Model                — dunder protocols, type-level lookup, operator dispatch
//   u4  Iteration and Generators      — iterator protocol, resumable frames, lazy pipelines
//   u5  Functions as Objects          — argument binding, def-time defaults, decorators
//   u6  Classes, MRO, and Descriptors — attribute precedence, C3, descriptors, metaclasses
//   u7  Exceptions and Resources      — exception model, finally, context managers, groups
//   u8  Data Structures and Cost      — dynamic arrays, hash tables, sorting, complexity
//   u9  Concurrency                   — GIL and atomicity, asyncio, choosing a model
//   u10 Types, Contracts, and Tests   — gradual typing, variance, protocols, property tests

