              "name": "Rebinding versus mutation",
              "statement": "Let `x` and `y` be names bound to the same object o (so `x is y` holds). Then:\n\n(1) After executing `x = z` for any expression `z`, the name `y` is still bound to o, and o is unchanged. The statement affects exactly one namespace entry.\n\n(2) After executing any operation that mutates o through `x` (`x.append(...)`, `x[i] = ...`, `x.attr = ...`), the object reachable through `y` has the new value, and `x is y` still holds.\n\nConsequently no in-place mutation can ever break an alias, and no rebinding can ever propagate through one.",
              "proof": "(1) By the definition of binding, `x = z` evaluates `z` to some object o′ and replaces the entry for the name `x` in its namespace with a reference to o′. The operation is defined entirely in terms of the name `x` and the object o′; it does not read, write, or even inspect the entry for `y`, and it performs no operation on o. Hence `y`'s entry, still referring to o, is unchanged, and o's value is unchanged.\n\n(2) A mutating operation invoked as `x.append(v)` first evaluates `x`, obtaining o, then invokes a method whose receiver is o. By the definition of identity, an object's identity is fixed for its lifetime, so no mutation replaces o with a different object; it alters o's *value* in place. The name `y` still refers to o, so any subsequent read through `y` reads o's current — that is, the new — value. Since neither name's binding was touched, `x is y` continues to hold. ∎\n\nThe proposition is the entire content of the aliasing bug: the author of `pending = orders` believed they had performed (1) and were therefore safe, while their subsequent `pending.remove(...)` performed (2)."
