              "body": "Here is a failure that looks impossible. You build a `Point` class, give it a sensible `__eq__` so that two points with the same coordinates compare equal, and use points as dictionary keys. Everything works, until one day a lookup for a key you are *certain* is present raises `KeyError` — and printing the dictionary shows a key that compares equal to the one you just looked up sitting right there in it.\n\nNothing is broken. The dictionary is behaving exactly as specified; the class violated a contract that the specification depends on. This lesson states that contract precisely, proves why hash tables cannot work without it, and derives the practical rules — when to define `__eq__`, what you owe `__hash__` when you do, and why mutable objects make dangerous keys — from the proof rather than from folklore. Along the way it separates the two questions people habitually conflate: *are these the same object?* and *do these objects mean the same thing?*"
