              "name": "Identity comparison and equality comparison",
              "statement": "**Identity.** `x is y` evaluates to `True` exactly when `x` and `y` denote the same object, i.e. `id(x) == id(y)`. It is not overridable: no special method can change what `is` means. It is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive for every object without exception.\n\n**Equality.** `x == y` is a protocol invocation. Python calls `type(x).__eq__(x, y)`; if that returns the sentinel `NotImplemented`, it tries the reflected operation `type(y).__eq__(y, x)`; if that also declines, the result falls back to `x is y`. The default `object.__eq__` declines everything except identity, so a class that defines no `__eq__` compares equal only to itself.\n\nEquality is *whatever the type says it is*. The language does not enforce that `__eq__` is reflexive, symmetric, or transitive — it only assumes it, and container types (`dict`, `set`, `list.index`, `in`) are written under that assumption."
