                  "hint": "Compare what `__eq__` looks at with what `__hash__` looks at.",
                  "solution": "It violates **(H1)**. `__eq__` compares field values, so two separately constructed `Version(1, 0)` objects are equal; `__hash__` returns the identity, which is distinct for distinct objects, so their hashes differ. By the hash-consistency theorem, inserting one and looking up the other walks a different probe sequence and reports absence — a cache that silently never hits and grows without bound. The tests pass because they compare with `==` and never round-trip a key through a dict, or because they happen to reuse the same object.\n\nMinimal fix: hash exactly the fields `__eq__` compares — `def __hash__(self): return hash((self.major, self.minor))`. Two secondary defects are worth fixing at the same time: `__eq__` raises `AttributeError` on any non-`Version` operand instead of returning `NotImplemented`, and the class is mutable, so (H2) holds only by convention. `@dataclass(frozen=True, order=True)` discharges all three at once. ∎"
