                  "hint": "What must become immutable, and what must the equality be defined over?",
                  "solution": "**Design.** Make the key a frozen value object over normalized, immutable components:\n```\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass RequestKey:\n    method: str                            # normalized upper-case\n    url: str                               # normalized (scheme/host lower-cased)\n    params: tuple[tuple[str, str], ...]    # sorted items — NOT a dict\n```\nconstructed via `RequestKey(m.upper(), normalize(u), tuple(sorted(params.items())))`.\n\n**(H1)**: `@dataclass(frozen=True)` generates `__eq__` and `__hash__` from the *same* field list, so consistency holds by construction and survives someone adding a field later. **(H2)**: `frozen=True` makes assignment raise `FrozenInstanceError`, so no field the hash reads can change while the key is in the table; the parameter `dict` — mutable and unhashable — is converted at the boundary into a sorted tuple, which also makes two requests with the same parameters in different insertion orders compare equal, as they should.\n\n**Why `hash(str(request))` is a defect.** It conflates the key with one particular *rendering* of it. It is fragile — any `__repr__`/`__str__` change silently invalidates every cache entry, so a formatting edit becomes a cache-correctness change. It is order-sensitive if the string embeds a dict, so equivalent requests miss. And unless `__eq__` is defined over the same string it violates (H1) outright. Worse, it invites hash-only comparison — treating a hash collision as equality — and hashes are 64-bit and lossy, so distinct requests will eventually collide and one will be served the other's cached response. The hash locates; only `==` decides. ∎"
