                  "solution": "**Non-termination.** Let `a = [1]; a.append(a)`, so `a[1] is a`. A memo-free deep copy of a would copy element 0, then recursively deep-copy element 1 — which is a itself — invoking the same procedure on the same object with no state distinguishing the two invocations. The recursion is infinitely regressive and raises `RecursionError` (or diverges). The memo terminates it: before recursing, `deepcopy` records `memo[id(a)] = new_a`, so the inner call finds `id(a)` already present and returns the partially-built `new_a`, reproducing the cycle in the copy — `b[1] is b` — rather than unrolling it.\n\n**Semantic wrongness.** Let `shared = [0]; pair = [shared, shared]`, so `pair[0] is pair[1]`. A memo-free deep copy would copy `shared` twice, yielding `cp[0] is not cp[1]`: two independent lists where the original had one. That is not a faithful copy — mutating `cp[0]` no longer shows up in `cp[1]`, so the copy does not behave like the original under any subsequent program.\n\nThe general statement: the memo makes `deepcopy` an *isomorphism of the reachable object graph* — preserving both cycles and sharing — rather than an unrolling of it into a tree. Any correct deep-copy implementation needs identity-keyed memoization for exactly this reason, which is also why deep copies cannot be cheap: the procedure must remember every object it has already visited. ∎"
