              "body": "You build a list of callbacks in a loop — one per button, one per retry policy, one per worker — and every one of them behaves as though it were the last. The code looks obviously correct:\n\n```\nhandlers = [lambda: print(i) for i in range(3)]\nfor h in handlers: h()      # prints 2, 2, 2 — not 0, 1, 2\n```\n\nThis is the single most reported Python 'gotcha' after mutable defaults, and it is not a gotcha at all: it is the defining property of closures, visible. A closure does not capture a *value*; it captures a **cell** — a mutable box shared with the scope that created it — and it reads that cell **when it is called**, not when it is created. Once you have that sentence, the output above is the only possible output, and every standard fix is an application of the same rule.\n\nThis lesson defines cells precisely, proves the late-binding property, shows how to inspect the machinery from the REPL, derives the three correct fixes and why each works, and ends with the equivalence between closures and single-method objects that tells you when to use which."
