              "type": "theorem",
              "kind": "definition",
              "name": "Free variable, cell, and closure",
              "statement": "Let g be a function block nested inside a function block f. A name n is a **free variable** of g if n is referenced in g, is not local to g, and is local to some enclosing function block (here f).\n\nWhen the compiler detects that a local of f is a free variable of some nested block, it does not give f an ordinary local slot for n. Instead it marks n as a **cell variable** of f (`f.__code__.co_cellvars`) and a **free variable** of g (`g.__code__.co_freevars`). At runtime, each *activation* of f allocates a **cell object** — a one-slot mutable container — for each cell variable; f's reads and writes of n go through that cell.\n\nA **closure** is a function object together with a tuple of cells, one per free variable, stored on the function object as `g.__closure__`. Constructing the function object at `def`/`lambda` evaluation time copies references to the enclosing activation's cells into that tuple. Reading a free variable in g's body compiles to `LOAD_DEREF`, which dereferences the cell **at the moment the instruction executes**."
