              "type": "theorem",
              "kind": "theorem",
              "name": "Late binding of free variables",
              "statement": "Let g be a closure with free variable n captured from activation A of the enclosing block. Then every evaluation of n inside a call to g yields the object stored in A's cell for n **at the time of that call**. In particular, if the enclosing block rebinds n after g is created, later calls to g observe the new object; and if k closures are created in one activation with the same free variable, all k observe the same object at any instant.",
              "proof": "By the definition above, creating the function object g copies a reference to A's cell for n into `g.__closure__` — it copies the *cell*, not the cell's contents, and the function object never stores the value of n. A read of n in g's body compiles to `LOAD_DEREF i`, which at execution time fetches `g.__closure__[i]` and returns that cell's current contents. Since the instruction executes during the call, its result is the cell's contents at call time.\n\nA rebinding of n in the enclosing block — or in any closure that declared `nonlocal n` — compiles to `STORE_DEREF`, which writes the *same* cell. Therefore any write that happens between g's creation and a call to g is visible to that call.\n\nFor the k-closures claim: all k function objects are created during the same activation A, and A allocated exactly one cell per cell variable. Each `MAKE_FUNCTION` copies a reference to that one cell, so all k closure tuples hold the same cell object, and all reads dereference it. Hence at any instant they all observe the same object. ∎\n\nApplying this to the opening example: the comprehension's iteration variable `i` is a cell variable of the (implicit) comprehension scope, rebound on each iteration through the same cell; the three lambdas capture that one cell; by the time any of them is *called*, the loop has finished and the cell holds 2. Printing 2, 2, 2 is not a bug — it is the theorem."
