              "type": "text",
              "heading": "The three fixes, and why each one works",
              "body": "Every correct fix for the loop-capture problem does one of two things: it either gives each closure **its own cell**, or it stops using a cell and stores the value **on the function object** instead. There is no third mechanism, so knowing the two makes the fixes derivable rather than memorized.\n\n**Fix by default argument** — `lambda i=i: print(i)`. Default arguments are evaluated once, at function-definition time, and stored on the function object (`__defaults__`). The parameter `i` is now a *local* of the lambda, not a free variable, so no cell is involved and the value is frozen at creation. This is the cheapest fix and the reason the idiom exists, though it does put an extra parameter in the signature that a caller could override.\n\n**Fix by factory function** — `def handler(i): return lambda: print(i)`, called as `handlers = [handler(i) for i in range(3)]`. Each call to `handler` creates a *new activation*, hence a new cell for `i`, hence a distinct capture. This is the most explicit fix and generalizes to any number of captured variables.\n\n**Fix by partial application** — `functools.partial(print, i)`. This binds the argument into an object at creation time, bypassing closures entirely. It is the right choice when the closure was only ever going to forward its captures as arguments.\n\nNote what is *not* on the list: there is no way to ask a closure to capture by value after the fact. The capture strategy is fixed by how the inner function is written."
