              "heading": "`import X` versus `from X import y` — a binding difference, not a style difference",
              "body": "Both forms execute X exactly once; they differ only in step 5, and that difference has consequences no style guide can override.\n\n`import X` binds one name to the *module object*. Every use is `X.y`, an attribute lookup performed **at call time**, so it sees whatever `X.y` is at that moment. `from X import y` binds the name `y` in your namespace to *the object `X.y` denotes right now* — one snapshot, taken at import time, of the object rather than of the module slot.\n\nThree consequences follow directly. **Rebinding a module attribute later is invisible** to anyone who did `from X import y` — this is the immutable-name aliasing of Unit 1 wearing a different hat, and it is why `unittest.mock.patch` insists you patch *where the name is looked up* rather than where it is defined. **Circular imports break asymmetrically**: `from X import y` needs `y` to already exist on a partially-executed X, while `import X` only needs the module object, which step 3 guarantees exists. **Rebinding module state for testing works** with `import X` and silently does nothing with `from`. None of this makes `from` wrong — it makes it a snapshot, and snapshots of *immutable* objects (functions, classes, constants that never get reassigned) are exactly as good as the module reference."
