              "heading": "Worked example: diagnosing and fixing a circular import",
              "body": "```\n# a.py\nfrom b import B          # line 1\nclass A:\n    def make(self): return B()\n\n# b.py\nfrom a import A          # line 1\nclass B:\n    def make(self): return A()\n```\n`import a` produces: *ImportError: cannot import name 'A' from partially initialized module 'a' (most likely due to a circular import)*.\n\n**Why, exactly.** Step 3 registers an empty module `a` and starts its body. Line 1 imports `b`, which registers empty `b` and starts *its* body. `b`'s line 1 does `from a import A`: step 1 finds `'a'` in `sys.modules` — a **hit**, so `a` is not re-executed — and step 5 performs `getattr(module_a, 'A')`. But `a`'s body has executed only up to its own line 1, so `A` has not been bound yet. The attribute lookup fails, and the error message names the real cause.\n\n**Fix 1 — use the module reference, not the attribute.** Change `b.py`'s import to `import a` and its body to `return a.A()`. Now step 5 binds only the module object, which exists (empty but registered), and the attribute lookup is deferred to *call* time, by which point both bodies have finished. This is the smallest fix and works whenever the cycle is only needed at runtime, not at class-definition time.\n\n**Fix 2 — import inside the function.** Move `from a import A` into `B.make`'s body. Same deferral, more localized, at the cost of a (cached, cheap) import call per invocation.\n\n**Fix 3 — break the cycle.** Extract the shared abstraction into `c.py` that neither imports the other, or invert the dependency so one side takes a factory as a parameter. This is the only fix that removes the fragility rather than routing around it, and it is what a cycle is usually telling you: two modules that must know each other's concrete types are one module, or are missing a third.\n\n**Non-fix.** Reordering the imports \"so a is imported first\" merely relocates which side of the cycle fails, and reordering statements inside the module makes correctness depend on line numbers across files."
