              "heading": "Packages, `__init__.py`, and the shape of a dependency graph",
              "body": "A **package** is a module whose source is a directory: importing `pkg` executes `pkg/__init__.py` with `pkg.__path__` set to the directory, and `import pkg.mod` first fully imports `pkg`, then executes `pkg/mod.py` and binds `mod` as an attribute of the `pkg` module object. So `import pkg.mod` always runs `pkg/__init__.py` first — which is why heavy imports there make every submodule import expensive, and why an `__init__.py` that eagerly imports its submodules for convenience creates a cycle the moment a submodule imports a sibling.\n\nTwo mechanics follow. **Relative imports** (`from . import sibling`, `from ..core import thing`) are resolved against `__package__`, so they work regardless of where the package sits on `sys.path` — and they fail with `ImportError: attempted relative import with no known parent package` when the file is run directly as a script, which is the same `__main__`-naming trap as before. **Namespace packages** (PEP 420) allow a directory without `__init__.py` to act as a package split across `sys.path` entries; useful for plugin distribution, and a source of confusion when a missing `__init__.py` silently produces a package that shadows the one you meant.\n\nThe design advice the import model implies is simple: keep the module dependency graph a DAG, put shared types at the bottom of it, keep `__init__.py` thin (re-exports and nothing expensive), and let `if TYPE_CHECKING:` imports (Unit 10) carry the type-only edges that would otherwise force a cycle."
