                  "prompt": "A codebase has `jobs/worker.py` containing `class Job` and a module-level `REGISTRY = {}`. Developers run it with `python jobs/worker.py`; a web process imports `jobs.worker`. In production, `isinstance(obj, Job)` returns False for objects the worker created, and the registry seen by the web process is empty. Explain the mechanism, and give the fix plus a check that would have caught it in CI.",
                  "hint": "What is the cache key, and how many keys does this file end up under?",
                  "solution": "**Mechanism.** The `sys.modules` cache is keyed by module *name*, not by file path. `python jobs/worker.py` executes the file under the name `__main__`; the web process's `import jobs.worker` finds no entry for `'jobs.worker'` (only `'__main__'` is present, and nothing links them) and therefore executes **the same file a second time** under a second key. Import idempotence holds per name, and there are now two names, so the theorem's converse applies: two module objects, each with its own `REGISTRY` dict and its own `Job` **class object**.\n\nThat explains both symptoms. `isinstance(obj, Job)` compares an instance whose `__class__` is `__main__.Job` against the class `jobs.worker.Job` — two distinct objects, so the check is False even though the source is identical. And entries added to `__main__.REGISTRY` are invisible in `jobs.worker.REGISTRY`, so the web process sees an empty dict.\n\n**Fix.** Ensure the file is only ever reached under one name. Put the entry point behind `if __name__ == '__main__': main()` so the module body has no side effects when run as a script, and launch with `python -m jobs.worker`, which imports the module under its real dotted name and executes it as `__main__` *by that name's module object*. Better still, move the entry point to a separate thin `jobs/__main__.py` or a console-script entry point, so the module holding shared state is never the script.\n\n**CI check.** Assert the invariant directly: a test that imports `jobs.worker` and asserts `sys.modules['jobs.worker'].Job is Job` after exercising the worker path, or a start-up assertion `assert __name__ != '__main__' or not REGISTRY`, catching state populated under the wrong name. A lint rule banning executable top-level side effects outside a `__main__` guard prevents the class of bug rather than this instance. ∎"
