            "type": "proof",
            "prompt": "Prove that `[lambda: i for i in range(n)]` produces n functions that all return the same value, while `[lambda i=i: i for i in range(n)]` produces n functions returning 0…n−1. Your proof must argue from what is stored on the function object in each case and when the value is read, not from observed output.",
            "rubric": [
              "States what a closure captures — a cell (a mutable one-slot box owned by the enclosing activation) — and that reads of a free variable compile to a deref executed at call time.",
              "Establishes that all n lambdas in the first form are created in a single activation, which allocates exactly one cell for the loop variable, so all n closure tuples reference the same cell object.",
              "Concludes that after the loop the cell holds the final value, so every call dereferences that one cell and returns the same value.",
              "Shows the second form makes the name a parameter (a local, not a free variable), so no cell is involved and the default is evaluated once per iteration at function-creation time and stored on that function object.",
              "Notes the distinguishing observable evidence (e.g. __closure__ shared vs None, __defaults__ populated) or equivalently identifies the factory-function alternative as creating one activation — hence one cell — per closure."
            ],
            "solution": "**First form.** The comprehension is compiled as a single function-like scope, so all n lambdas are created during **one activation** of that scope. The loop variable `i` is referenced inside the lambdas but is not local to them, so it is a free variable of each lambda and a cell variable of the comprehension scope. Frame setup for that one activation allocates exactly **one** cell object for `i`; each iteration's `MAKE_FUNCTION` builds the lambda's `__closure__` tuple by copying a reference to that same cell (observable: `fs[0].__closure__[0] is fs[1].__closure__[0]` is True). Each iteration then rebinds `i` by writing the *same* cell.\n\nA read of `i` inside a lambda compiles to `LOAD_DEREF`, which fetches the cell's contents at the instant the instruction executes — i.e. when the lambda is **called**, not when it was created. All calls happen after the comprehension has finished, at which point the single cell holds the last value produced, n−1. Therefore all n functions return n−1: one cell, read late. ∎\n\n**Second form.** `i=i` makes `i` a **parameter** of the lambda. By the local-determination rule, a parameter is local to the lambda's block, so `i` is no longer a free variable there and no cell is created for it (observable: `fs[0].__closure__ is None`). The default value expression `i` on the right-hand side is evaluated in the *enclosing* scope **once, at function-creation time**, and the resulting object is stored in that function object's `__defaults__` tuple. Since a new function object is created on each iteration, the k-th one stores the value of `i` as of iteration k, i.e. k. Calling it with no arguments binds the parameter to that stored default. Therefore the functions return 0, 1, …, n−1: n independent snapshots, read from per-function storage. ∎\n\nThe factory alternative — `def make(i): return lambda: i` — reaches the same result by the other mechanism: each call to `make` is a distinct activation allocating its own cell, so there are n cells rather than one, and late binding is harmless because nothing rebinds them.",
            "explanation": "The graded skill is deriving behaviour from storage. A complete proof identifies *one activation ⇒ one cell ⇒ shared, read-at-call-time* for the first form, and *parameter ⇒ no cell ⇒ value stored per function object at creation* for the second. Answers that say 'closures capture by reference, defaults capture by value' state the conclusion without the mechanism and cannot then explain why a factory function also works. The strongest answers cite the inspectable evidence (`__closure__` identity vs `None`, `__defaults__`), because that is what turns the argument into something checkable at a REPL.",
