              "type": "text",
              "heading": "Two methods, and your class is a sequence",
              "body": "Here is a claim that sounds too strong and is exactly true: implement `__len__` and `__getitem__`, and your class immediately supports iteration, the `in` operator, `list()`, tuple unpacking, slicing, `random.choice`, `reversed()`, and every function in the standard library documented as taking \"a sequence\" — without inheriting from anything and without registering anywhere.\n\nThat leverage comes from **fallback chains**: the built-in operations do not demand one specific method, they try a sequence of protocols in order, so a partial implementation still lands somewhere useful. Knowing the chains is what lets you answer, precisely, why your class works with `for` but not with `in`, why `len()` is O(1) on one type and absent on another, and — the practically important one — why implementing `__contains__` can turn an O(n) membership test into O(1) while changing nothing else about your class.\n\nThis lesson states the container protocols, proves the two fallback rules from the operations that implement them, and then addresses the design question they raise: when to implement methods yourself, when to inherit mixins from `collections.abc`, and why subclassing `list` or `dict` is usually the wrong way to get container behaviour."
