              "kind": "definition",
              "name": "The container protocols",
              "statement": "**Iterable.** A type is *iterable* if `iter(x)` succeeds — which requires `__iter__` returning an iterator, or (by the fallback below) `__getitem__` accepting consecutive integers from 0.\n\n**Iterator.** A type is an *iterator* if it defines both `__next__`, returning the next item or raising `StopIteration`, and `__iter__` returning `self`. The second requirement is what makes an iterator usable directly in a `for` loop and is why `iter(iterator) is iterator`.\n\n**Sized.** `__len__` returning a non-negative `int`; enables `len(x)` and participates in truthiness (lesson 1).\n\n**Container.** `__contains__(self, item)` returning a bool; enables `x in c` in O(whatever you implement).\n\n**Sequence.** Sized + iterable + `__getitem__` accepting integers **and slice objects**, with integer indices from 0 to len−1 and negative indices counting from the end; conventionally also `index` and `count`.\n\n**Mapping.** `__getitem__` by arbitrary key, `__len__`, `__iter__` over keys, `__contains__` over keys; conventionally `keys`, `values`, `items`, `get`.\n\nNo declaration makes a type a member of any of these — the methods do."
