              "heading": "collections.abc: mixins and membership tests in one hierarchy",
              "body": "The ABCs in `collections.abc` do two jobs at once, and separating them clarifies when to use which.\n\nAs **mixins**, they trade abstract methods for concrete ones. Inherit from `Sequence` and implement just `__len__` and `__getitem__`, and you receive `__contains__`, `__iter__`, `__reversed__`, `index`, and `count`, each defined in terms of the two you supplied. Inherit from `MutableMapping` and implement `__getitem__`, `__setitem__`, `__delitem__`, `__iter__`, `__len__`, and you receive `get`, `pop`, `popitem`, `clear`, `update`, `setdefault`, `keys`, `values`, `items` — a large, correct, consistent API for five methods. The mixin implementations are generic and therefore not always optimal (the inherited `__contains__` is the O(n) scan), so override the ones whose cost matters. This is the strongest reason to inherit rather than merely conform.\n\nAs **type tests**, they answer `isinstance(x, Iterable)` structurally: their metaclass implements `__subclasshook__`, so any class defining `__iter__` is recognized as an `Iterable` without inheriting from it, and `Sequence.register(MyClass)` can assert conformance for classes that cannot inherit. Two cautions: the hooks check only for the *presence* of methods, never their behaviour or signatures, so `isinstance(x, Sequence)` is a claim about shape and not about correctness; and `isinstance(x, Iterable)` is False for a class relying only on the `__getitem__` fallback, even though `for x in obj` works — the hook tests `__iter__`, and the fallback lives in `iter()`, not in the type. When you need a static, structural check, `typing.Protocol` (Unit 10) is the modern tool; `collections.abc` remains the right runtime answer."
