              "type": "theorem",
              "kind": "corollary",
              "name": "Iterators are one-shot; the second loop is empty",
              "statement": "Let `it` be an iterator. Then `for x in it:` immediately after a completed `for x in it:` executes its body zero times, and `list(it)` after `list(it)` returns `[]`. By contrast, if `c` is an iterable that is not an iterator, both loops run in full.",
              "proof": "By the `for`-statement theorem, each loop begins with `iter(it)`. Since `it` is an iterator, its `__iter__` returns `self`, so both loops drive **the same object**. The first loop ran until `__next__` raised `StopIteration`; by the exhaustion-is-permanent rule, every subsequent `__next__` also raises `StopIteration`. The second loop therefore receives `StopIteration` on its first `FOR_ITER` and exits with zero body executions. `list(it)` is the same argument, since `list` builds its result by driving `iter(it)` to exhaustion.\n\nFor an iterable that is not an iterator, `iter(c)` constructs a *new* iterator on each call — a `list_iterator` holding a fresh index, for instance — so the second loop starts from the beginning and the corollary's premise fails. ∎\n\nThis is why `map`, `filter`, `zip`, `enumerate`, `reversed`, generator objects, file objects, `csv.reader`, and DB cursors are all consumed by a single pass, while `list`, `tuple`, `dict`, `set`, `range`, and `str` are not. The diagnostic is one line: `iter(x) is x` — True means one-shot."
