            "id": "u4q5",
            "type": "proof",
            "prompt": "Prove that a pipeline of k non-materializing generator stages over a source of n items, consumed by an O(1) reducer, uses auxiliary space independent of n — and then prove that replacing any single stage with a list comprehension makes the peak Θ(n). Your argument must be inductive over the stages and must state the non-materializing hypothesis explicitly.",
            "rubric": [
              "States the non-materializing hypothesis: each stage's own state is O(1) in the stream length and it holds at most a constant number of items at a time.",
              "Gives a base case: the source iterator holds O(1) state (file buffer, index, counter), not the whole stream.",
              "Gives the inductive step: stage i holds a reference to stage i−1 plus O(1) state, and items pulled and discarded are immediately unreferenced by the pipeline, so no accumulation occurs.",
              "Concludes O(k) total with no term in n, and notes that time is unchanged (Θ(n) per stage) — laziness buys space and early termination, not speed.",
              "Proves the list-comprehension case: a materializing stage must construct the whole list before the next stage's first item, so at that instant Θ(n) items are simultaneously referenced, giving a Θ(n) peak regardless of the other stages' laziness."
            ],
            "solution": "**Setup.** Let the pipeline be s → g₁ → … → g_k → R, where s is the source iterator, each gᵢ is a stage, and R is the reducer. The **non-materializing hypothesis** on each gᵢ is: gᵢ's own state is O(1) in n, and any single `gᵢ.__next__()` call holds at most a constant number of items from its input simultaneously, retaining none after it returns.\n\n**Base case.** The source holds O(1) state: a file object holds a fixed-size read buffer plus an offset; a `list_iterator` holds a reference to the (pre-existing) list and an integer index, contributing O(1) *auxiliary* space; `range`/`count` hold an integer.\n\n**Inductive step.** Assume stages g₁…g_{i−1} together hold O(i−1) auxiliary space. Stage gᵢ holds a reference to g_{i−1} (O(1)) plus its own state, which is O(1) by hypothesis. When R drives the pipeline, a call to `gᵢ.__next__()` pulls some bounded number of items from g_{i−1}; each is either returned onward or discarded, and gᵢ retains no reference to a discarded item, so it becomes unreachable from the pipeline immediately and is eligible for reclamation. Hence gᵢ adds O(1) and the total after i stages is O(i).\n\n**Conclusion.** After k stages plus an O(1) reducer, the peak auxiliary space is O(k), which contains no term in n. Note that **time is unchanged**: each of the n items still crosses each of the k stages once, giving Θ(nk) work, typically with a slightly larger constant than the eager version because each crossing is a Python-level iterator call rather than a step in a tight C loop. Laziness buys space and the ability to terminate early, not speed. ∎\n\n**The materializing case.** Suppose stage gⱼ is replaced by a list comprehension `L = [f(x) for x in g_{j−1}]`. By the semantics of a list display, the comprehension runs to completion before the expression's value exists: it drives g_{j−1} to exhaustion, appending each result to L. Immediately before the next stage receives its first item, L holds up to n items, all simultaneously referenced by the name L, so the live set is Θ(n) and the peak auxiliary space is Θ(n). This holds regardless of how lazy the other k−1 stages are — the peak is determined by the maximum over time of the live set, and one materializing stage supplies a Θ(n) point. In the worst case where every stage materializes and all results remain named, the peak is Θ(nk). ∎",
            "explanation": "The graded skill is arguing about the *live set over time* rather than gesturing at 'generators are memory-efficient'. A complete proof states the hypothesis (which is what excludes `sorted`, `tee`, and `list`), inducts over the stages, and explains why discarded items do not accumulate (nothing in the pipeline retains a reference). The second half must identify that a materializing stage forces a Θ(n) *instant* — the peak is a maximum over time, so one eager stage defeats the laziness of all the others. Answers that claim laziness also improves speed have the wrong model: it moves work, it does not remove it.",
