              "body": "Unit 2 used defaults deliberately: `lambda i=i: print(i)` fixes the loop-capture problem precisely *because* the default is evaluated at definition time, once per lambda, and stored on that function object rather than read from a shared cell. That is not a hack around the trap — it is the same mechanism used for its actual semantics, and it is worth recognizing as a legitimate technique with a clear signature: a parameter whose name shadows an enclosing variable and which callers are not expected to pass.\n\nTwo related idioms rest on the same footing and deserve more scepticism. Some code writes `def f(x, _len=len):` to turn a global lookup into a local one — measurably faster in a hot loop, since locals are array indices and globals are dict lookups (Unit 2). It also adds a parameter that a caller can override, pollutes the signature, and is worth roughly nothing outside a genuinely hot loop; measure before believing it. Others use a default to memoize (`def f(x, _cache={})`) — which works, for exactly the reason the trap works, but hides the cache in the signature where `help()` displays it, instead of using `functools.cache`.\n\nThe judgment to carry away: a default is *storage attached to a function object*. Use it when you want per-definition storage — capturing a loop variable, freezing a value — and never when you want per-call computation."
