                  "solution": "```\n[1, 2, 3] [1, 2, 3] [1, 2, 3]\n[1] [2] [3]\n```\n\n**`a`** — one list object is created at def-time and stored in `a.__defaults__`. Each call binds `acc` to that object (binding never copies) and **mutates** it with `append`, so the accumulation persists across calls. The three printed values are all the *same object*, which is why they are identical: `print` evaluates all three arguments before printing, and by then the shared list contains 1, 2, 3.\n\n**`b`** — the same single list is stored and bound. But `acc = acc + [x]` calls `list.__add__`, which **constructs a new list**, and then rebinds only the local name `acc` (Unit 1's clause 1). The stored default is never mutated, so it remains `[]` for every call, and each call returns its own fresh one-element list.\n\n**The precise rule this demonstrates:** the trap is not caused by a mutable default per se, but by a mutable default that the body **mutates**. `b` has a mutable default and no bug. The safe formulation is 'never mutate a default', with 'use only immutable defaults' as the easy-to-follow approximation — and the correct fix for `a` remains the sentinel, since a caller who omits the argument almost certainly wants a fresh list. ∎"
