              "heading": "The standard-library decorators whose semantics have teeth",
              "body": "`functools.lru_cache(maxsize=128)` and `functools.cache` (an unbounded alias) memoize on the *arguments*, and three consequences follow from that alone. Arguments must be **hashable**, so a function taking a list or dict cannot be cached without a conversion. The cache **holds strong references** to every key and value, so an unbounded `@cache` on a function called with many distinct arguments is a memory leak with a friendly name — and on a *method*, the key includes `self`, so the cache pins every instance it has ever seen for the life of the process (the standard fix is `functools.cached_property` for per-instance caching, or an explicit cache keyed by an id). And equal-but-distinct arguments share an entry by the hash contract (Unit 1), so a mutable argument that is mutated after caching produces a stale hit — the same failure as a mutable dict key.\n\n`functools.singledispatch` turns a function into a generic function dispatching on the *first argument's type*, with `@fn.register` adding implementations. It is the clean answer to a chain of `isinstance` checks, and it dispatches on the runtime type using the MRO (Unit 6), so a subclass gets the base's implementation unless it registers its own.\n\n`property`, `staticmethod`, and `classmethod` are decorators only incidentally: they return **descriptor** objects, and their real semantics live in the attribute-lookup protocol of Unit 6. The practical consequence here is placement — they must be the outermost decorator, because what they return is not a plain callable and another decorator wrapping them would break the descriptor behaviour."
