            "id": "u5q6",
            "type": "open",
            "prompt": "You are reviewing a PR adding an `@audited` decorator to ~200 functions. It is written as a nested `wrapper(*args, **kwargs)` with no `functools.wraps`, reads its configuration as `@audited(level=settings.AUDIT_LEVEL)`, and sets `wrapper.audited = True` so a startup check can verify coverage. CI is green. Identify the defects, explain each from the mechanisms in this unit, and state what you would require before approving.",
            "rubric": [
              "Identifies the missing functools.wraps and its functional (not merely cosmetic) consequences — introspecting frameworks seeing (*args, **kwargs), pickling/multiprocessing failure by __qualname__, and loss of attributes set by other decorators via __dict__.",
              "Identifies that the coverage marker is set on the wrapper, so it is invisible to any decorator applied above it and is lost if another decorator without wraps wraps it — making the startup check unreliable in exactly the stacked cases.",
              "Identifies the import-time capture of settings.AUDIT_LEVEL by the decorator expression, so runtime reconfiguration and test patching have no effect and the value depends on import timing.",
              "Explains why CI is green — tests exercise call behaviour, not introspection, pickling, or reconfiguration — and proposes concrete tests that would fail against the current code.",
              "States the required changes: functools.wraps, reading configuration at call time or injecting it, and a coverage check based on __wrapped__ or a registry rather than an attribute on the wrapper."
            ],
            "solution": "**Defect 1 — no `functools.wraps`.** The name is bound to `wrapper`, an ordinary function object whose metadata describes itself. Consequences that are functional rather than cosmetic:\n- `inspect.signature` reports `(*args, **kwargs)`, so any framework that introspects to inject arguments (pytest fixtures, FastAPI dependencies, Click, `functools.singledispatch`) sees the wrong shape and either errors or silently passes nothing. Across 200 functions, some of them will be in such a framework.\n- `__qualname__` points at the decorator's inner function, so `pickle` cannot re-import the function — which breaks `multiprocessing`, Celery, and any callable-caching layer.\n- `wrapper.__dict__` is empty, so any attribute another decorator attached *below* `@audited` (route markers, `__isabstractmethod__`, task flags) is dropped. That silently un-registers routes and silently makes abstract methods concrete.\n- Logs, profilers, and error grouping report every one of the 200 functions as `wrapper`, aggregating unrelated failures together.\n\n**Defect 2 — the coverage marker is on the wrong object.** `wrapper.audited = True` marks the *outermost object `@audited` produced*. If any decorator is applied above it, the startup check sees that outer object; unless *it* used `wraps` (which copies `__dict__`), the marker is invisible and the check reports the function as unaudited — or, worse, if the check is 'warn only', it reports coverage that does not correspond to reality. So the audit-coverage guarantee is strongest exactly where it is least needed (unstacked functions) and unreliable where stacking is involved.\n\n**Defect 3 — configuration frozen at import.** `@audited(level=settings.AUDIT_LEVEL)` is a decorator *expression*, evaluated when each `def` statement executes — at import time. The integer is then closed over by the wrapper. Rebinding `settings.AUDIT_LEVEL` later changes one module attribute that nothing reads again (Unit 1: rebinding does not propagate through aliases), so runtime reconfiguration silently does nothing and tests that patch the setting are ineffective. Because the value is captured at first import, it also depends on import order (Unit 2's import idempotence), which makes the level nondeterministic across test suites.\n\n**Why CI is green.** The tests almost certainly assert *call behaviour* — that the decorated function returns the right value and that an audit record is written. None of the three defects change call behaviour. They change introspection, serialization, marker propagation, and reconfiguration, none of which a behavioural test exercises.\n\n**What I would require.**\n1. `@functools.wraps(fn)` on the wrapper — one line, and it fixes defects 1 and (partly) 2, since `wraps` copies `__dict__` upward through any stack of well-behaved decorators.\n2. Read the level at call time — `level = self_level if self_level is not None else settings.AUDIT_LEVEL` inside the wrapper — or inject a config object at decoration. State which contract is intended (frozen-at-import vs live) and test it.\n3. Base the coverage check on something the stack cannot hide: register the function in a module-level registry at decoration time (`AUDITED.add(fn.__qualname__)`), or walk `__wrapped__` chains in the check. An attribute on one object in a stack is not a reliable marker.\n4. Tests that fail against the current code, because that is what makes them worth adding: `assert inspect.signature(decorated) == inspect.signature(original)`; `pickle.dumps(decorated)`; a stacked case asserting a below-marker survives; and a test that patches `settings.AUDIT_LEVEL` and asserts the new level is used.\n5. Given the blast radius of 200 call sites, I would also want a note on the per-call overhead of an extra frame in any hot path, and confirmation that the wrapper's `try/finally` records audits even when the wrapped function raises.",
            "explanation": "All three defects share a shape: they are invisible to behavioural tests because they concern *metadata and timing*, not results. A strong review names the mechanism for each — wrapper metadata is the wrapper's own; a marker set on one object in a stack is not visible to objects above it; a decorator expression runs at import — and then proposes tests that fail on the current code, which is what separates a review from a list of preferences. The subtlest point is defect 2: `wraps` partly rescues the marker by copying `__dict__`, but only if *every* decorator in the stack uses it, so a registry is the robust answer.",
