              "heading": "`__getattr__` versus `__getattribute__`",
              "body": "The two hooks differ in when they run, and confusing them causes infinite recursion.\n\n**`__getattr__(self, name)`** runs **only after** the normal algorithm has raised `AttributeError`. It is therefore a *fallback*: it costs nothing for attributes that exist, and it is the right tool for proxies, lazy loading, and dynamic attributes (`row.customer_id` on a record wrapper). Inside it, `self.other` is safe as long as `other` exists, because the normal algorithm handles it.\n\n**`__getattribute__(self, name)`** runs for **every** attribute access, before anything else. Overriding it intercepts everything — including `self.anything` inside your own override, which is why the naive implementation recurses until the stack dies. The only safe body delegates: `return object.__getattribute__(self, name)` or `super().__getattribute__(name)`. Reserve it for genuine whole-object interception (auditing, recording proxies) and expect a real performance cost, since it defeats the interpreter's per-type attribute caches.\n\nTwo further traps. Special methods invoked implicitly by syntax bypass **both** hooks, because they are looked up as type slots (Unit 3) — so a proxy that forwards via `__getattr__` will forward `obj.foo()` but not `len(obj)` or `obj[k]`; those must be defined explicitly on the proxy class. And `hasattr(obj, 'x')` is defined as 'does `getattr` succeed', so a `__getattr__` that raises the wrong exception type — `KeyError` instead of `AttributeError` — makes `hasattr` propagate rather than return `False`, and breaks `copy`, `pickle`, and `inspect`, all of which probe for optional dunder attributes."
