                  "solution": "**Proof.** `cached_property` implements `__get__` and neither `__set__` nor `__delete__`, so it is a **non-data descriptor**, found at step 1 and eligible only at step 4.\n\n*First access.* Step 2's test fails (not a data descriptor). Step 3 consults `obj.__dict__`, which has no entry for the name, so it misses. Step 4 invokes `T.__get__(obj, cls)`, whose body computes the value, executes `obj.__dict__[self.attrname] = value`, and returns it. The `__set_name__` hook is what supplied `attrname`, so the entry is written under exactly the name the attribute is accessed by.\n\n*Every later access.* Step 2 still fails. Step 3 now finds the entry written on the first access and returns it — and because step 3 precedes step 4, the descriptor is never consulted again. The getter therefore runs exactly once per instance, with no bookkeeping in the getter itself: the caching is a consequence of *precedence*, not of logic. ∎\n\n**Condition 1 — the instance must have a `__dict__`.** With `__slots__` and no `'__dict__'` slot, step 3 has nothing to consult and the descriptor has nowhere to store the value, so the very first access raises `TypeError: No '__dict__' attribute on 'C' instance to cache 'v' property.` — it fails immediately rather than silently recomputing. `cached_property` is fundamentally incompatible with slots.\n\n**Condition 2 — it is not thread-safe.** The write is a plain dict assignment guarded by nothing; CPython 3.12 removed the class-wide lock that previously serialized first accesses (it was a scalability bottleneck). Two threads may both miss at step 3, both run the getter, and both write — so an expensive or side-effecting getter can execute more than once. For an idempotent pure computation that is merely wasteful; for anything with side effects it is a bug, and an explicit lock is required.\n\nA third, milder caveat: because the cached value lives in the instance dict, `del obj.attr` invalidates the cache (which is the supported way to force recomputation), and any code that replaces `obj.__dict__` wholesale silently resets it. ∎"
