              "statement": "Inside a method of class C, the zero-argument `super()` is compiled to `super(C, self)`, where C is the class **whose body the call textually appears in** (supplied by the compiler as an implicit `__class__` closure cell — Unit 2's cells, used by the language itself).\n\n`super(C, self).name` looks up `name` in the classes **after C** in `type(self).__mro__` — not in `C.__bases__`. Since `type(self)` may be any subclass, the class that a given `super()` call reaches is **not determined by the source file containing it**; it depends on the runtime type of the instance.\n\nThe two-argument form `super(SomeClass, obj)` is occasionally needed explicitly — in a `classmethod` (`super(C, cls)`), outside a class body, or when deliberately starting the search from a different point."
