                  "solution": "Write o for `object`. L[O] = [O, o]; L[A] = [A, O, o]; L[B] = [B, O, o]; L[C] = [C, O, o].\n\n**L[D] = D + merge([A,O,o], [B,O,o], [A,B])**\n1. Head A — not in any tail. Emit A. → merge([O,o], [B,O,o], [B])\n2. Head O — in the tail of [B,O,o]? Yes. Reject. Head B — in no tail. Emit B. → merge([O,o],[O,o],[])\n3. Emit O, then o.\n**L[D] = [D, A, B, O, object]**\n\n**L[E] = E + merge([B,O,o], [C,O,o], [B,C])** — by the same steps, **L[E] = [E, B, C, O, object]**\n\n**L[F] = F + merge(L[D], L[E], [D,E]) = F + merge([D,A,B,O,o], [E,B,C,O,o], [D,E])**\n1. Head D — not in any tail. Emit D. → merge([A,B,O,o], [E,B,C,O,o], [E])\n2. Head A — not in any tail. Emit A. → merge([B,O,o], [E,B,C,O,o], [E])\n3. Head B — in the tail of [E,B,C,O,o]? Yes. Reject. Head E — in no tail. Emit E. → merge([B,O,o], [B,C,O,o], [])\n4. Head B — in the tail of [B,C,O,o]? Its tail is [C,O,o] — no. Emit B. → merge([O,o],[C,O,o])\n5. Head O — in the tail of [C,O,o]? Yes. Reject. Head C — no. Emit C. → merge([O,o],[O,o])\n6. Emit O, then o.\n**L[F] = [F, D, A, E, B, C, O, object]**\n\n**Verifying monotonicity** on the pair (B, C): in L[E] = [E, B, C, O, o], B precedes C. In L[F], B is at index 4 and C at index 5 — B still precedes C. ✓ Verifying local precedence: F's bases D and E appear in L[F] at indices 1 and 3, in declared order. ✓ (Note E appears *before* B even though E is a later base than D — required because B is a base of E, and a class must never precede its own subclass.) ∎"
