                  "hint": "Build a diamond and trace both versions.",
                  "solution": "**The hierarchy.**\n```\nclass Base:\n    def save(self): print('Base')\nclass Audited(Base):\n    def save(self): print('Audited'); Base.save(self)     # explicit\nclass Compressed(Base):\n    def save(self): print('Compressed'); super().save()\nclass Doc(Audited, Compressed): pass\n\n>>> Doc.__mro__\n(Doc, Audited, Compressed, Base, object)\n>>> Doc().save()\nAudited\nBase                      # Compressed was SKIPPED — no compression happened\n```\nThe explicit call jumps straight to `Base`, bypassing everything between `Audited` and `Base` in the MRO. The bug is silent: the document saves, and nobody notices it was never compressed. Had `Compressed.save` also used an explicit `Base.save(self)` and been reached by some other path, `Base.save` would run *twice* — the dual failure.\n\n**Proof that `super()` cannot skip.** With `Audited.save` calling `super().save()`, the call resolves to `super(Audited, self)`, which searches `type(self).__mro__` strictly after `Audited` — i.e. `(Compressed, Base, object)` — and takes the first class defining `save`, namely `Compressed`. Its own `super()` searches strictly after `Compressed`, reaching `Base`. Because each step searches only *after* the current class and the search index strictly increases, no class between the caller and the terminus can be bypassed, and none can be revisited. Every class in the MRO that defines the method is therefore executed exactly once, in order. ∎\n\n**The three rules for a cooperative method.**\n1. **Always `super()`, never a named base.** An explicit base call encodes a static parent, while the actual chain is a property of `type(self)` and is unknown to the class's author.\n2. **Accept and forward `**kwargs`**, consuming only your own (keyword-only) parameters, so classes later in the MRO still receive theirs. Positional arguments cannot be selectively consumed and force every class to know its successors' arity.\n3. **Ensure termination.** Some class must stop calling `super()` — either a designated base, or `object`'s implementation for `__init__`/`__new__` (which accepts no extra arguments, so rule 2 must have consumed them all by then)."
