              "body": "A function acquires a lock, does work, releases the lock. It is three lines and it is wrong, because between acquisition and release the code can leave by paths its author did not enumerate: an exception from any called function, an early `return` added later by someone else, a `break` out of an enclosing loop, a `KeyboardInterrupt` between any two bytecodes. Each missed path leaks the lock, and a leaked lock is a deadlock the next time round.\n\nThe general problem is that correctness of cleanup requires reasoning about *every* exit from a block, and human enumeration of exits is unreliable and decays under maintenance. Python's answer is to make the guarantee structural rather than enumerative: `try/finally` promises that a block runs on every exit, and the `with` statement packages an acquire/release pair so that neither can be written without the other.\n\nThis lesson proves both guarantees, derives the exact desugaring of `with` (so that `__enter__`/`__exit__` stop being mysterious), shows how `@contextmanager` turns a generator's suspension point into an acquire/release boundary — a direct application of Unit 4 — and covers the two traps: `return` inside `finally`, and relying on `__del__`."
