              "body": "`@contextmanager` converts a generator into a context manager by mapping the two protocol methods onto generator operations (Unit 4): `__enter__` runs the generator up to its single `yield` and returns the yielded value; `__exit__` either resumes it with `next()` (normal exit) or **throws the exception in at the yield point** with `gen.throw()`. That last detail is the whole reason cleanup must be inside a `try` in the generator body — an exception in the `with` body materializes *at the `yield`*, and code after a bare `yield` is skipped exactly as it would be after any raise.\n\nThree rules follow. **Yield exactly once**: yielding twice raises `RuntimeError: generator didn't stop`, and never yielding raises `RuntimeError: generator didn't yield`. **Put cleanup in `finally`** (or handle the exception explicitly, as `transaction` does) — cleanup written after the `yield` without protection runs only on the success path. **A generator-based manager is single-use**: the generator is exhausted after one `with`, so `cm = transaction(conn)` followed by two `with cm:` blocks raises. The class form has no such restriction, which is why `threading.Lock` is a class.\n\nTo suppress an exception from the generator form, `return` normally inside the `except` — do not re-raise. But note the asymmetry with the class form: `__exit__` suppresses by returning truthy, and a generator manager suppresses by *not* re-raising, which is easy to do by accident. A `except Exception: pass` inside a `@contextmanager` silently swallows every failure in every `with` block that uses it."
