                  "solution": "**Closure on every path.** Expand:\n```\nmgr = open(p); exit = type(mgr).__exit__; value = type(mgr).__enter__(mgr)\nhit_except = False\ntry:\n    f = value; BODY\nexcept:\n    hit_except = True\n    if not exit(mgr, *sys.exc_info()): raise\nfinally:\n    if not hit_except: exit(mgr, None, None, None)\n```\n*Normal completion:* BODY falls through, `hit_except` is False, the finally clause calls `exit`. *Exception:* the bare `except` catches everything (including KeyboardInterrupt, since it is bare), sets the flag, calls `exit` with the exception info, and re-raises because `file.__exit__` returns None (falsy); the finally clause then sees the flag and does not call `exit` again. *return/break/continue in BODY:* these bypass the `except` but are covered by the `finally` (try/finally clauses 3 and 4), so `exit` runs with `(None, None, None)`. In every case `exit` is called, and the `hit_except` flag makes the two call sites mutually exclusive — hence **exactly once**. `file.__exit__` calls `close()`, so the descriptor is released. ∎\n\n**If `__exit__` were looked up on the instance.** It would be found via ordinary attribute lookup, so an instance-dict entry could shadow the class's method — meaning two objects of the same type could have different cleanup, and code could not reason about `with SomeType(...)` from the type alone. It would also make the protocol inconsistent with every other implicit invocation (Unit 3), and would break for types with `__slots__` or C-level implementations. The type-level rule is what lets a reader conclude 'this `with` closes the file' from the class.\n\n**If `__exit__` were fetched after `__enter__`.** An object with `__enter__` but no `__exit__` would *acquire the resource* and only then raise `AttributeError` — leaving it acquired with no way to release it, which is precisely the failure the statement exists to prevent. Fetching first makes the malformed-manager error occur before any side effect. ∎"
