              "code": "import random, time\n\ndef with_retry(fn, *, attempts=4, base=0.2, cap=10.0,\n                retry_on=(ConnectionFailed, ConnectTimeout, ServerError)):\n    \"\"\"Retry ONLY failures that are transient AND safe to repeat.\n\n    The caller is asserting fn is idempotent by passing it here.\n    \"\"\"\n    for attempt in range(1, attempts + 1):\n        try:\n            return fn()\n        except retry_on as e:\n            if attempt == attempts:\n                raise                                  # exhausted: propagate the LAST error\n            delay = min(cap, base * 2 ** (attempt - 1))\n            delay = random.uniform(0, delay)           # full jitter — avoids synchronized\n            log.warning('attempt %d/%d failed (%s); retrying in %.2fs',\n                        attempt, attempts, e, delay)   # retry storms across clients\n            time.sleep(delay)\n\n# What each piece prevents:\n#   retry_on=(...)  — retrying a ClientError (4xx) or a ValueError would loop on a\n#                     permanent failure; the tuple encodes 'transient AND idempotent'\n#   attempts        — an unbounded retry is an outage amplifier, not resilience\n#   exponential     — a fixed delay under load keeps the failing service saturated\n#   jitter          — without it, N clients retry in lockstep and re-create the spike\n#   raise on last   — swallowing the final failure hides the outage from the caller\n#   log per attempt — otherwise a slow success is indistinguishable from a fast one\n\n# NOT retryable without an idempotency key:\n#   charge_card(amount)              -> POST; a read timeout may mean it succeeded\n#   append_to_log(line)              -> duplicates on retry\n# Made retryable:\n#   charge_card(amount, idem_key=k)  -> server deduplicates on k\n#   os.replace(tmp, dest)            -> atomic and naturally repeatable"
