              "type": "text",
              "heading": "The data structure Python is built out of",
              "body": "Dictionaries are not merely a useful container in Python — they are the substrate the language runs on. Module namespaces are dicts (Unit 2), instance and class attributes are dicts (Unit 6), keyword arguments arrive as a dict (Unit 5), and every global name lookup is a dict probe. A change to dict performance is a change to the performance of every Python program, which is why the implementation has been optimized harder than anything else in CPython and why understanding it pays off broadly.\n\nUnit 1 established the *correctness* contract: a hash table locates a key by its hash, so equal keys must hash equally (H1) and a key's hash must not change while it is stored (H2). This lesson takes up the *performance and behaviour* side: how CPython lays out a dict, why lookup is expected O(1) and what makes it degrade, why insertion order is preserved and why that became a language guarantee, and what the layout implies for the choices you make — when a set beats a list, what `defaultdict` and `Counter` actually save, and why grouping with a dict beats sort-then-`groupby`.\n\nThe practical target is being able to predict, for any keyed workload, both the cost and the iteration order without measuring."
