              "type": "text",
              "heading": "Sorting twice to sort by two things",
              "body": "A report must list employees by department, and within each department by salary descending, and within equal salaries by name. Most people reach for a compound key — `key=lambda e: (e.dept, -e.salary, e.name)` — which works here and stops working the moment one of the fields is not numerically negatable (a descending sort on a string) or the comparison is not expressible as a tuple.\n\nThe general technique is different and depends on a property most people do not know they are relying on: **sort by the least significant key first, then by the next, and so on** — because Python's sort is *stable*, each pass preserves the ordering established by the previous one within ties. Two `sort` calls, no compound key, and each pass can have its own `reverse=`.\n\nThat is one payoff of understanding the ordering layer precisely. This lesson covers the rest: what Timsort is and why it is O(n) on the already-sorted data that dominates real workloads, why stability is a theorem you can build on rather than a coincidence, what a `key` function costs and when the decorate-sort-undecorate pattern is still needed, and the two structures — heaps and `bisect` — that solve ordering problems a full sort solves too expensively."
