              "kind": "definition",
              "name": "Stability, and Timsort",
              "statement": "A sort is **stable** if elements that compare equal retain their relative input order in the output. `list.sort` and `sorted` are guaranteed stable by the language.\n\n**Timsort** (Tim Peters, 2002; also adopted by Java, Android, V8, and Rust's `sort`) is an adaptive, stable merge sort:\n\n1. Scan for **natural runs** — maximal already-ascending or strictly-descending stretches (descending runs are reversed in place, which preserves stability because they are *strictly* descending, so no equal elements are swapped).\n2. Extend short runs to a minimum length (32–64) with **binary insertion sort**.\n3. Merge runs, maintaining a stack of pending runs under invariants that keep the merges balanced, using **galloping mode** — an exponential search — when one run is consistently winning.\n\nComplexity: **Θ(n) best case** (one run, i.e. sorted or reverse-sorted input), **Θ(n log n)** worst and average, Θ(n) auxiliary space. The adaptivity is the point: real data is very often partially ordered, and Timsort's cost degrades smoothly with disorder."
