              "statement": "Any sorting algorithm that gains information about the input only through pairwise comparisons must perform Ω(n log n) comparisons in the worst case. Timsort's Θ(n log n) worst case is therefore optimal up to constants, and its Θ(n) best case does not contradict the bound.",
              "proof": "Model the algorithm as a binary **decision tree**: each internal node is a comparison with two outcomes, and each leaf is an output permutation. To sort correctly, the algorithm must be able to produce every one of the n! permutations of a distinct-element input, so the tree has at least n! leaves. A binary tree with L leaves has height at least log₂L, and the height is the worst-case number of comparisons. Hence the worst case is at least\n\n  log₂(n!) ≥ log₂((n/e)^n) = n·log₂n − n·log₂e = Ω(n log n)\n\nusing Stirling's approximation. ∎\n\n**Why the best case escapes it.** The bound is on the *worst* case: it says some input requires Ω(n log n) comparisons, not every input. Sorted input is one path through the tree, and it may be short. Timsort detects a single ascending run in n−1 comparisons and stops — which is why sorting an already-sorted million-element list is milliseconds rather than seconds, and why appending to a sorted list and re-sorting is a legitimate (if slightly wasteful) technique rather than an outrage.\n\n**Why non-comparison sorts can beat it.** Counting sort and radix sort are Θ(n + k) and Θ(d·n) because they extract information from the keys' *structure* rather than by comparing — so the model's premise does not hold and the bound does not apply. For Python this matters mainly as an explanation of when to reach outside `sorted`: bucketing by an integer key with a dict is Θ(n) and beats sorting whenever the key space is small."
