              "body": "**(1) 'Find the slowest request.'**\n```\nslowest = sorted(requests, key=attrgetter('duration'))[-1]     # Θ(n log n)\nslowest = max(requests, key=attrgetter('duration'))            # Θ(n)\n```\nA full ordering is computed and then discarded except for one element. `max` does one pass with one comparison per element. This is the most common wasted sort in real code.\n\n**(2) 'Show the 20 slowest, out of 5 million.'**\n```\ntop = sorted(requests, key=..., reverse=True)[:20]             # Θ(n log n), Θ(n) space\ntop = heapq.nlargest(20, requests, key=...)                    # Θ(n log 20), Θ(20)\n```\n`nlargest` keeps a 20-element heap and compares each candidate against its minimum. Since log 20 is a constant, this is Θ(n) — and it accepts a generator, so the 5 million records need never be in memory at once (Unit 4).\n\n**(3) 'Order by department, then salary descending, then name.'**\n```\nrows.sort(key=lambda e: (e.dept, -e.salary, e.name))           # works only because\n                                                                # salary is numeric\nrows.sort(key=attrgetter('name'))                              # general: three stable\nrows.sort(key=attrgetter('salary'), reverse=True)              # passes, least\nrows.sort(key=attrgetter('dept'))                              # significant first\n```\nThe tuple version is fine here and fails as soon as a descending key is a string or a date. The three-pass version is general, and each pass after the first runs on nearly-sorted data where Timsort is close to linear.\n\n**(4) 'Which pricing tier applies to a usage of 8,400 units?'** given tier boundaries `[0, 1000, 5000, 20000]`.\n```\nfor i, lo in enumerate(bounds):                                # Θ(k) per query\n    if usage < lo: ...\ni = bisect.bisect_right(bounds, usage) - 1                     # Θ(log k) per query\n```\nWith k tiers and millions of queries, the difference is decisive — and `bisect` is also correct at the boundaries, where hand-written comparison chains routinely get `<` versus `<=` wrong. This is the case where the right tool is neither a sort nor a hash, and it is the one people most often miss."
