              "type": "callout",
              "tone": "danger",
              "body": "**Deadlock comes from lock *ordering*, not from lock count.** If thread A holds L1 and waits for L2 while B holds L2 and waits for L1, both stop forever, and the program simply hangs with no exception and no log line. The standard defence is a **global lock ordering**: define a total order over your locks (by name, by address, by an assigned rank) and require every code path to acquire in that order. Also prefer `with lock:` over manual acquire/release (Unit 7 guarantees the release on every exit path), never hold a lock across a blocking call you do not control, and use `RLock` only when a genuine re-entrant call chain requires it — not to paper over a design you have not analysed."
