              "body": "A thread costs about 8 MB of virtual address space for its stack and a kernel-scheduled context switch to hand off. Ten thousand concurrent connections handled by ten thousand threads is therefore possible and unpleasant. Ten thousand handled by ten thousand `asyncio` tasks costs a few kilobytes each and switches by resuming a Python frame — no kernel involvement — which is why async is the standard answer for high-concurrency I/O.\n\nBut the interesting property is not the memory saving; it is the **scheduling model**. Threads are preempted: a switch can happen between any two bytecodes (previous lesson), so any shared state can change under you at any moment. Tasks are **cooperative**: the event loop can only switch to another task when the current one *suspends*, and a task suspends only at an `await` that actually yields. That is a much stronger property, and it changes what you must defend against — and what you get for free.\n\nThis lesson defines the machinery (coroutines are Unit 4's resumable frames, with different syntax), proves the no-preemption-between-await-points theorem and its corollary about invariants, proves why a single blocking call starves every task in the process, and covers the two things that go wrong in real async code: races *across* awaits, and cancellation."
