              "body": "The theorem's corollary defines the entire discipline: **the state you read before an `await` may be stale after it.** Every check-then-act bug from Unit 7 reappears, with the window marked in the source.\n\n```\nasync def transfer(accounts, src, dst, amount):\n    if accounts[src] >= amount:            # check\n        await log_transfer(src, dst, amount)   # <-- SUSPENDS: other tasks run here\n        accounts[src] -= amount            # act on a stale check\n        accounts[dst] += amount\n```\nTwo concurrent transfers from the same account can both pass the check before either performs the debit, overdrawing it. The fix is the same as with threads — either eliminate the suspension from the critical section (do the logging after the state update, so check and act are adjacent and the no-preemption theorem covers them), or protect it with an `asyncio.Lock`, whose `async with` suspends rather than blocking the loop.\n\nThe practical technique is a code-reading rule: **to audit a critical section, look for `await` inside it.** If there is none, the theorem guarantees exclusion for free. If there is one, ask what could change during it and whether the code re-validates afterwards. This is a genuinely easier analysis than the threaded equivalent, where the danger is everywhere and invisible — and it is the strongest argument for preferring async over threads for I/O-heavy shared-state work.\n\nOne caution: `asyncio.Lock`, `Queue`, and `Event` are **not thread-safe** and coordinate tasks on one loop only. Crossing a thread boundary needs `queue.Queue` (in the thread direction) or `loop.call_soon_threadsafe` / `asyncio.run_coroutine_threadsafe` (in the loop direction); using the wrong one is a race that no amount of async discipline will catch."
