                  "solution": "**(a) Sequential — ~10 s.** Each `await` runs its coroutine to completion before the next iteration begins. The comprehension creates one coroutine at a time and immediately drives it; nothing is ever pending concurrently. This is the single most common async mistake: the code is full of `async`/`await` and performs identically to the blocking version.\n\n**(b) Concurrent — ~1 s.** `gather` wraps each coroutine in a Task and schedules them all on the loop before awaiting; while one is suspended on I/O, the others run. Total time ≈ the slowest fetch, not the sum.\n\n**(c) Nothing happens — ~0 s, and a warning.** Calling a coroutine function only creates coroutine objects (Unit 4: no body code runs at call time). Without an `await` or `create_task`, none is ever driven, so no request is made. Python emits `RuntimeWarning: coroutine 'fetch' was never awaited` — which is the diagnostic for this bug, and a good reason to run with warnings visible.\n\n**(d) Concurrent — ~1 s, with better failure semantics.** `tg.create_task` schedules each immediately; the `async with` block awaits all of them on exit. Unlike (b), a failure cancels the siblings rather than leaving them running, and all failures are reported together as an `ExceptionGroup`. This is the form to prefer in new code. ∎"
