              "type": "text",
              "heading": "What processes cost, and how to pay less",
              "body": "Every argument and every result crosses a process boundary by **pickling**, and that has three consequences worth planning around.\n\n**Not everything is picklable.** Lambdas, local functions, closures, open files, sockets, locks, database connections, and generators cannot cross. Functions are pickled by qualified name and re-imported in the child (Unit 2), which is why a worker function must be defined at module level and why `__main__`-guarded code matters: with the `spawn` start method the child **re-imports the module**, so any module-level side effect runs again in every worker.\n\n**Serialization is not free.** Shipping a 200 MB DataFrame to eight workers costs 1.6 GB of copying plus the pickling CPU, which routinely exceeds the computation being parallelized. The standard fix is to pass *references* rather than data — a path, an id, an offset range — and let each worker read its own share. When data genuinely must be shared, `multiprocessing.shared_memory` gives a zero-copy buffer, and memory-mapped files work well for read-only inputs.\n\n**Start-up costs milliseconds.** A pool amortizes it across many tasks; creating a process per item does not. And with `spawn`, the child pays a full interpreter start plus module imports — so a pool of 8 workers importing a heavy scientific stack can take seconds to become useful. Chunk the work (`pool.map(fn, items, chunksize=…)`) so each task is large enough that the per-task overhead is negligible; the default chunk size is often far too small for cheap items, and a `map` over a million tiny items can spend all its time on IPC.\n\nThe start method itself matters for correctness, not just speed. `fork` copies the parent's memory but **not its threads**, so a fork in a process that holds a lock in another thread inherits a permanently locked mutex — a hang with no traceback. This is why CPython moved away from `fork` as the Linux default in 3.14 (to `forkserver`), and why mixing `multiprocessing` with threads requires deliberate care about start method and fork timing."
