              "body": "A nightly job downloads 50,000 images, resizes each, and writes them to storage. Wall-clock: 6 hours. Three attempted fixes, and what measurement said about each.\n\n**Attempt 1 — `ProcessPoolExecutor` over the whole job.** Result: 5.5 hours, marginally better. *Why:* profiling showed 85% of the time was the downloads, and the process pool parallelized the downloads too — but each worker was doing one blocking download at a time, so with 8 workers the download stage went 8× faster while paying pickling costs on the image bytes returned. Amdahl with p = 0.85 and N = 8 caps this at 4.4×, and the serialization overhead ate most of it.\n\n**Attempt 2 — `ProcessPoolExecutor` with more workers.** Result: 6.5 hours, *worse*. *Why:* 64 processes on 8 cores contended for CPU and memory bandwidth, each holding a decoded image; the machine started swapping. The model's optimism is exactly the trap — it predicts a monotone improvement, and reality has a peak.\n\n**Attempt 3 — split the stages by their bottleneck.** Result: 25 minutes.\n```\nasync def download_all(urls, out_queue):\n    async with httpx.AsyncClient(limits=Limits(max_connections=200)) as client:\n        async with asyncio.TaskGroup() as tg:\n            for u in urls:\n                tg.create_task(download_one(client, u, out_queue))\n\ndef resize_stage(paths):                            # CPU-bound, in worker processes\n    with ProcessPoolExecutor() as pool:\n        yield from pool.map(resize_file, paths, chunksize=32)\n```\n*Why it works:* the download stage is pure waiting, so 200 concurrent async requests overlap it into roughly the time of the slowest few (limited by the server, not by us) — no processes, no pickling. The resize stage is pure CPU, so it goes to a process pool that receives **paths** rather than image bytes, making the pickling cost negligible, with `chunksize=32` so per-task IPC overhead is amortized. A bounded queue between the stages provides backpressure, so a fast downloader cannot exhaust memory ahead of a slower resizer.\n\n**The generalizable lesson.** The first two attempts applied one model to a job with two different bottlenecks. Measurement — a profile showing 85% I/O and 15% CPU — is what identified the split, and it is the step both failed attempts skipped. *Match the model to the stage, not to the program.*"
