                  "prompt": "For each workload, choose a model and justify it in one or two sentences:\n\n(a) A CLI that fetches 40 API endpoints and prints a summary.\n(b) A service handling 20,000 concurrent websocket connections.\n(c) Computing SHA-256 for 100,000 local files.\n(d) A pandas pipeline over a 4 GB CSV.\n(e) A scraper making 5,000 requests, each followed by a heavy HTML parse.",
                  "hint": "In each case ask where the wall-clock time is spent.",
                  "solution": "**(a) `ThreadPoolExecutor` with ~20 workers.** Almost all the time is network wait, and blocking I/O releases the GIL, so the waits overlap. At 40 endpoints the per-thread cost is irrelevant, and threads work with any blocking client — no rewrite, no event loop, a five-line change. `asyncio` would also work but adds a dependency on async clients for no benefit at this scale.\n\n**(b) `asyncio`.** 20,000 threads would need ~160 GB of stack address space and would thrash the kernel scheduler; 20,000 tasks cost a few tens of megabytes and switch by resuming a Python frame. Websockets are also long-lived and mostly idle, which is exactly the profile tasks are built for.\n\n**(c) `ProcessPoolExecutor`.** Hashing is CPU-bound Python/C work that holds the GIL, so threads give no speedup. Pass **paths**, not file contents, so each worker reads its own file and the pickling cost stays negligible; use `chunksize` to amortize IPC. (A free-threaded build would make threads viable here; so would `hashlib` releasing the GIL for large buffers, which it does — worth measuring first.)\n\n**(d) Neither — push it into the library.** pandas releases the GIL for many operations and does its own vectorization; the highest-leverage change is usually chunked reading (`chunksize=`), better dtypes (categoricals), or switching to Polars/DuckDB, which parallelize internally. Adding process-level parallelism means serializing DataFrames across pipes — often slower than the computation. Measure before assuming Python-level concurrency is the answer.\n\n**(e) Both, split by stage.** Async or threads for the 5,000 requests (pure waiting), feeding a `ProcessPoolExecutor` for the parses (pure CPU) via `run_in_executor` — passing the HTML text, which is small enough to pickle cheaply, or the path if it was written to disk. One model for the whole job would leave one of the two stages bottlenecked. ∎"
