            "type": "proof",
            "prompt": "Prove that mutable generic containers must be invariant, and that read-only views may be covariant. Then state and justify the API rule that follows for parameter and return annotations.",
            "rubric": [
              "States the definition of subtyping (substitutability without a well-typed program observing a difference) as the property to be contradicted.",
              "Constructs the covariance counterexample explicitly: a function taking list[Base] writes a sibling type, and the caller's well-typed use of its list[Derived] fails at runtime.",
              "Rules out contravariance too, via the read position, and concludes invariance is the only remaining option for a container with both read and write operations.",
              "Shows why read-only views escape: with no write operation the counterexample cannot be constructed, so the type parameter appears only in output positions and covariance is sound.",
              "States the API rule (accept the most general type usable, return the most specific produced) and justifies it from the theorem rather than as a style preference."
            ],
            "solution": "**Setup.** S <: T means a value of S may be used wherever a T is expected without any well-typed program observing a difference. To refute a proposed subtyping it suffices to exhibit a program that type-checks under the assumption and misbehaves at runtime.\n\n**Covariance for a mutable container is unsound.** Assume `list[Dog]` <: `list[Animal]`. Then:\n```\ndef add_a_cat(animals: list[Animal]) -> None:\n    animals.append(Cat())          # well-typed: Cat <: Animal, append takes Animal\n\ndogs: list[Dog] = [Dog()]\nadd_a_cat(dogs)                     # well-typed by the assumption\ndogs[0].bark()                      # well-typed: elements are declared Dog\n```\nEvery line type-checks, yet at runtime `dogs` holds a `Cat` and `bark()` raises `AttributeError`. A well-typed program observed a difference, contradicting the definition. ∎\n\n**Contravariance is also unsound.** Assume `list[Animal]` <: `list[Dog]`. Then a `list[Animal]` whose first element is a `Cat` may be passed where a `list[Dog]` is expected, and the recipient's well-typed `xs[0].bark()` fails. ∎\n\n**Hence invariance.** A mutable container has a read position (`__getitem__ -> T`) and a write position (`append(T)`). The first argument rules out covariance and the second rules out contravariance, so `list[S]` <: `list[T]` only when S = T.\n\n**Read-only views may be covariant.** `Sequence[T]` provides `__getitem__`, `__len__`, `__iter__`, `index`, `count` — and no `append`, `__setitem__`, or `extend`. The type variable therefore occupies only *output* positions, so the poisoning counterexample cannot be constructed: there is no operation by which a `Cat` could enter the container. The only remaining obligation is that reading a `Dog` where an `Animal` was expected be safe, which is exactly what Dog <: Animal licenses. Hence `Sequence[Dog]` <: `Sequence[Animal]`, and likewise for `Iterable`, `Collection`, and `frozenset`. ∎\n\n**The API rule.** *Accept the most general type your body can work with; return the most specific type your body actually produces.*\n\nIt follows directly. For **parameters**, function subtyping is contravariant, so widening a parameter can only make your function usable in *more* contexts — and since `list` is invariant while `Iterable`/`Sequence` are covariant, declaring `Iterable[Item]` instead of `list[Item]` accepts `list[SubItem]`, tuples, generators, and cursors without any caller conversion. Every narrowing you impose is O(n) work or a rejected call at some caller you have never met. For the **return**, covariance means a more specific type is always substitutable where a general one was promised, so returning `list[User]` rather than `Iterable[User]` gives callers indexing, `len`, and repeated iteration at no cost to you (you built a list anyway). The rule is not stylistic: it is the theorem applied to your own signature.",
